            <div xml:id="d881">

                <pb facs="0830" n="820" sameAs="frus:frus1941v05/pg820"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.002/1136: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-10-28T18:00:00+09:00">October 28, 1941—6
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received November 11:17 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1698. Embassy’s 1528, September 29, 11 a.m.,<anchor n="63" xml:id="d881-f1"/>
                    Chinese Maritime Customs. A reply to our representations has been received from
                    the Foreign Office in note No. 122 of October 24. The general position of the
                    Japanese Government as communicated in the Embassy’s 1422, September 8, 4 p.m.
                    is overstated [<hi rendition="#italic">restated?</hi>]. Although no direct
                    reference is made to the demand for the appointment of a Commissioner of Customs
                    at Shanghai (specifically mentioned in our representations), the note states
                    that the Japanese Government supports the contentions of the Japanese
                    authorities in Shanghai. Full translation of note appears in the immediately
                    following telegram.<anchor n="64" xml:id="d881-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>Sent to the Department via Shanghai. Shanghai please repeat to Chungking and
                    Peiping.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
